WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.867 --> 00:00:02.102
- I mean it was an escalation,

00:00:02.102 --> 00:00:04.804 align:start size:88% position:13%
because you had not just
you know, one person,

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:08.308 align:start size:88% position:13%
Trump or his supporters
being ghosted

00:00:08.308 --> 00:00:11.611 align:start size:97% position:3%
or deplatformed but you
had 16 million Parler users

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:12.879
or whatever it is.

00:00:12.879 --> 00:00:16.016
99.9% of whom never did
anything wrong you know?

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:19.619
And that was a diminishment
of their free speech rights.

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:23.223
It was also I think unjustified,

00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:28.228 align:start size:97% position:3%
if the justification for
that was that Parler was used

00:00:29.429 --> 00:00:32.632
in the planning of the
events of January 6th,

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:34.200
it was clearly a scapegoating

00:00:34.200 --> 00:00:38.138 align:start size:84% position:16%
because we now have
FBI charging documents

00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:39.439 align:start size:72% position:28%
have come out.
- Yeah.

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:42.675 align:start size:84% position:16%
- 226 cases, the
social networking site

00:00:42.675 --> 00:00:46.212
that was used 10x more than
all the others was Facebook.

00:00:46.212 --> 00:00:48.481
You know Parler was used
one tenth of the amount

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:49.983
that Facebook was but,

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:53.753
Parler was you know, was
sort of an easy target

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.522
to sort of scapegoat for that.

00:00:55.522 --> 00:00:57.891
(calm music)

00:01:02.095 --> 00:01:03.696 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- I'm Dave Rubin
and joining me today

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:06.266 line:0%
is the former COO of PayPal,

00:01:06.266 --> 00:01:09.069 line:0%
an angel investor in such
companies as Facebook,

00:01:09.069 --> 00:01:10.804
Uber and SpaceX.

00:01:10.804 --> 00:01:13.706
The founder of Craft
Ventures and a cohost

00:01:13.706 --> 00:01:16.242
of the the All-In Podcast.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:18.778
David Sacks, welcome
to The Rubin Report.

00:01:18.778 --> 00:01:19.746
- Great to be here.

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:21.181
- I'm glad to have you.

00:01:21.181 --> 00:01:24.017 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know when I was looking
through all your bios

00:01:24.017 --> 00:01:26.786 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Wikipedia which I'm
sure is all perfectly true

00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:28.388
and all that good stuff,

00:01:28.388 --> 00:01:30.657 align:start size:84% position:16%
there's a lot of stuff
I could've pulled
there for the bio

00:01:30.657 --> 00:01:33.626 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I was thinking for my
young audience watching this,

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:36.429 align:start size:97% position:3%
like basically being a
part of all of those things

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:39.432
kind of seems like the
coolest thing possible.

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:42.702
So how does somebody
get into all of that?

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:48.208 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- Yeah I mean I was lucky
to graduate Stanford in 1994

00:01:48.208 --> 00:01:49.909 line:0%
which was the year
before the internet

00:01:49.909 --> 00:01:53.012
really kind of took off
and I was lucky enough

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:55.248
to meet Peter Thiel
when I was there

00:01:55.248 --> 00:01:57.584
and we collaborated together on,

00:01:57.584 --> 00:02:00.653
you know we both worked
on the student newspaper,

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:02.355 align:start size:97% position:3%
the sort of conservative
libertarian student newspaper

00:02:02.355 --> 00:02:03.823
that Peter founded
and then we ended up

00:02:03.823 --> 00:02:06.292 align:start size:88% position:13%
writing a book together
and he recruited me

00:02:06.292 --> 00:02:09.796
years later to join
PayPal and you know,

00:02:09.796 --> 00:02:12.632 align:start position:0%
actually he thought I had
missed the whole internet thing

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:15.034
and then I got a call
from Peter in 1999

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:18.638
and he told me about this
company he was creating

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:21.975
and you know, that company
went on to become PayPal

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:23.943
and he recruited
me to be the COO.

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:24.878 align:start size:75% position:25%
- Do you think?
- So yeah,

00:02:24.878 --> 00:02:26.112
that's how I got into it.

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:28.448
- Yeah do you think
Peter or you or Elon,

00:02:28.448 --> 00:02:31.351
like did anyone realize
what any of this stuff

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:34.087 align:start size:88% position:13%
was gonna become or just
when it's unfolding,

00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:37.157
you just kind of run with
it and see what happens?

00:02:37.157 --> 00:02:39.159
- You know, it, at the time,

00:02:39.159 --> 00:02:42.228 align:start size:81% position:19%
we sort of knew
on a certain level

00:02:42.228 --> 00:02:44.097
that it was changing the world.

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:47.133 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like at PayPal we
had this user counter

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:48.368
that would track the user growth

00:02:48.368 --> 00:02:50.937
and we called it the
World Domination Index.

00:02:50.937 --> 00:02:53.940
So on a certain level
we kind of you know,

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.441 align:start size:84% position:16%
thought it was
taking over the world

00:02:55.441 --> 00:02:58.611
but on another level we
didn't quite you know,

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:00.113
understand it.

00:03:00.113 --> 00:03:01.614 align:start size:94% position:6%
It felt a little bit like
fantasy or something.

00:03:02.815 --> 00:03:05.018
So yeah, it was like you know,

00:03:05.018 --> 00:03:07.387 align:start size:84% position:16%
it definitely operated
on both levels.

00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:09.789
- Yeah, how much of PayPal
was just trying to get people

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:13.393 align:start size:97% position:3%
to like understand you
could do things differently.

00:03:13.393 --> 00:03:17.430
I literally remember the
first time I saw PayPal,

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:19.632 align:start position:0%
I had moved into a new apartment
with a couple of roommates,

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:22.202
I was a struggling comic,
I barely had any money

00:03:22.202 --> 00:03:23.903 align:start size:84% position:16%
and one of my
roommates said to me,

00:03:23.903 --> 00:03:26.739 align:start size:94% position:6%
you've gotta pay me on
PayPal for the rent and I,

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.174
I didn't even know
what that meant.

00:03:28.174 --> 00:03:30.677 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like the idea that I
could link a bank account

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:32.312
to something online

00:03:32.312 --> 00:03:34.080 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I don't even
think we had WiFi yet.

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.615
It was still wire to wall.

00:03:35.615 --> 00:03:37.984 align:start size:97% position:3%
Just getting people to
understand there's new ways

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:40.220 align:start size:88% position:13%
of doing things, how
much of it is just that?

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:43.289
- A big part of it and then
the other big part of it

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:45.792
was just making it really
easy to actually do it.

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:48.428 align:start size:84% position:16%
So you know we, it's
a lot easier now.

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:49.829
We didn't have all
the tools you know,

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:52.031
that we have now 20 years ago

00:03:52.031 --> 00:03:55.301 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so just getting the
product to be super simple

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:56.803
so you could put it
in an email address,

00:03:56.803 --> 00:03:58.271 align:start size:81% position:19%
put it in your
credit card number

00:03:58.271 --> 00:04:00.440 align:start size:97% position:3%
and it would send the money
and then you'd get it,

00:04:00.440 --> 00:04:01.774
be able to take it
to your bank account.

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:03.443 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean it sounds really
easy but you know,

00:04:03.443 --> 00:04:04.777
there's all this
friction in the way

00:04:04.777 --> 00:04:06.246
that had to be removed

00:04:06.246 --> 00:04:07.747 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think part of the
reason why we're successful

00:04:07.747 --> 00:04:10.183 align:start size:94% position:6%
is we were able just to
make it incredibly simple.

00:04:11.317 --> 00:04:14.220
- Yeah so you mentioned
"The Diversity Myth"

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.489
and I've talked to
Peter on the show

00:04:16.489 --> 00:04:20.393
and you guys wrote
this book, 1995.

00:04:20.393 --> 00:04:23.162 align:start size:84% position:16%
In essence you wrote
this at Stanford.

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:26.332 align:start size:97% position:3%
You guys kind of predicted
everything that was happening,

00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:27.800
that's happening right now.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:31.738 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like really the idea that
sort of the diversity

00:04:31.738 --> 00:04:33.239
of immutable characteristics

00:04:33.239 --> 00:04:35.341
would matter more than
the diversity of thought

00:04:35.341 --> 00:04:37.043
which we now see just rampaging

00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:38.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
through all our businesses
and institutions

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:41.047 align:start size:84% position:16%
and the political
sphere and everything.

00:04:41.047 --> 00:04:43.416
How did you guys see it coming?

00:04:43.416 --> 00:04:47.387 align:start size:97% position:3%
We had been you know,
writing on the Stanford Revue

00:04:47.387 --> 00:04:49.289 align:start size:84% position:16%
and Stanford was sort
of the epicenter.

00:04:49.289 --> 00:04:53.960 align:start position:0%
That there was a famous protest
in 1988 where Jesse Jackson

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:56.996
led a mob that was chanting,

00:04:56.996 --> 00:04:59.065 align:start size:88% position:13%
"Hey hey, ho ho, western
culture's gotta go."

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:01.701 align:start size:84% position:16%
So Stanford was sort
of the epicenter

00:05:01.701 --> 00:05:05.838
for a lot of that stuff and
so we were writing about it

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:07.707
as student journalists in a way

00:05:07.707 --> 00:05:09.609
and that book was an extension

00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:11.911 align:start size:88% position:13%
of the student
journalism we had done.

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:14.414
You know, looking back,

00:05:14.414 --> 00:05:17.183 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean I was a teenager
when we wrote a lot of that.

00:05:17.183 --> 00:05:19.085 align:start size:97% position:3%
I wouldn't necessarily
make all the same arguments

00:05:19.085 --> 00:05:20.586
the same way that
I did back then

00:05:20.586 --> 00:05:22.822 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I do think we were
ahead of the curve

00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:27.827
in realizing you know, this,
this growing illiberalism.

00:05:28.861 --> 00:05:30.096 align:start size:84% position:16%
This sort of
political correctness,

00:05:30.096 --> 00:05:33.066
now it's called cancel
culture where you know,

00:05:33.066 --> 00:05:37.303
we're putting restrictions
on people's ability to speak

00:05:37.303 --> 00:05:40.873 align:start size:94% position:6%
and to think and to
express themselves in ways

00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:42.542
that I think would've been,

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:47.947
that certainly you
know, 1960s liberals

00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:51.784
wouldn't have you know,
wouldn't have supported

00:05:51.784 --> 00:05:54.320 align:start size:88% position:13%
and so you know, I never
actually saw myself

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:55.822
as a conservative.

00:05:55.822 --> 00:05:57.924 align:start size:94% position:6%
I saw myself as more of
like a 1960s-style liberal

00:05:57.924 --> 00:06:00.259
who believed in free
speech and you know,

00:06:00.259 --> 00:06:01.561
a colorblind society.

00:06:01.561 --> 00:06:03.730
- I know the feeling man.

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.965
- I think we're on a
similar journey here.

00:06:05.965 --> 00:06:07.900 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Yeah.
- With these issues.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:12.305 align:start size:94% position:6%
But yeah, I mean it was
the illiberalism on campus

00:06:12.305 --> 00:06:15.041
that sort of pushed me to become

00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:18.778
more politically
aware and you know,

00:06:18.778 --> 00:06:20.146 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that's what
the book was about.

00:06:20.146 --> 00:06:22.081 align:start size:84% position:16%
And now really, the
whole country is like,

00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:24.450 align:start size:81% position:19%
it was like Andrew
Sullivan says,

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:25.852
we all live on campus now.

00:06:25.852 --> 00:06:27.086
- Right, 'cause it's
kind of funny to me.

00:06:27.086 --> 00:06:29.789 align:start size:84% position:16%
You guys were writing
about this in 95

00:06:29.789 --> 00:06:32.692
and say when I was talking
about some of this stuff,

00:06:32.692 --> 00:06:34.160
five years ago.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
So you know, 20 years
after you wrote about it,

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:39.632
people kept saying to me,
no no it's just gonna stay

00:06:39.632 --> 00:06:43.336
on campuses and when they
get to the real world,

00:06:43.336 --> 00:06:47.039
the real world will
show them and no.

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:49.409
- Well those students.
- That did not work out.

00:06:49.409 --> 00:06:52.145
- Those students
graduated you know,

00:06:52.145 --> 00:06:55.548 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they took those
ideologies into the places

00:06:55.548 --> 00:06:58.885
where they went and so you know,

00:06:58.885 --> 00:07:00.420
they took them to the,

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:01.954 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know a lot of those
graduates went off

00:07:01.954 --> 00:07:06.025
to run school boards or to
run you know, newspapers,

00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:08.227
The New York Times
and places like that.

00:07:08.227 --> 00:07:12.732
I mean this is, it's a
very elite philosophy

00:07:12.732 --> 00:07:15.835
or ideology that's being imposed

00:07:15.835 --> 00:07:18.271
and it's coming from
places like Stanford

00:07:18.271 --> 00:07:21.107 align:start size:84% position:16%
and other Ivy
League type schools.

00:07:21.107 --> 00:07:22.742
- Yeah, so you've
been big on sort

00:07:22.742 --> 00:07:25.278
of the three topics that at
least for me at the moment

00:07:25.278 --> 00:07:28.614 align:start size:88% position:13%
are my big three topics
which are big tech,

00:07:28.614 --> 00:07:31.918 align:start size:88% position:13%
COVID and then
California specifically

00:07:31.918 --> 00:07:33.920 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause I think there's
COVID at a sort of national

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:35.488
and worldwide level
and then California.

00:07:35.488 --> 00:07:39.792 align:start position:0%
So we'll hold that for a moment
but on the big tech stuff,

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:42.361 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know I started
talking about free speech

00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:44.797
a couple years ago and
then there was suddenly

00:07:44.797 --> 00:07:47.567
this feeling that we were
kind of being censored

00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:49.068
but we couldn't figure it out.

00:07:49.068 --> 00:07:51.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
Then we started hearing
phrases like shadowbanning

00:07:51.804 --> 00:07:55.041
and deboosting and
algorithmic fairness,

00:07:55.041 --> 00:07:56.809
all of these things.

00:07:56.809 --> 00:07:58.811 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I think one of the things
that people wonder about

00:07:58.811 --> 00:08:03.549
is how this all infected all of,

00:08:03.549 --> 00:08:06.652
seemingly all of the
big tech companies.

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:09.255
Do you have any theories
on where it sort of started

00:08:09.255 --> 00:08:12.792
in there and how it
became so inclusive?

00:08:12.792 --> 00:08:14.360
- Yeah, well I think you know,

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:18.064 align:start size:88% position:13%
the technology industry
is primarily based
in Silicon Valley

00:08:18.064 --> 00:08:21.100 align:start size:84% position:16%
and most of the people
are pretty liberal

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:24.070 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so there would be
sort of that liberal skew

00:08:24.070 --> 00:08:28.040
but for I'd say most of
the evolution of these

00:08:28.040 --> 00:08:32.445 align:start position:0%
big tech companies they saw
themselves as neutral platforms

00:08:32.445 --> 00:08:35.348 align:start size:88% position:13%
and they didn't see
themselves as partisans

00:08:35.348 --> 00:08:37.517
engaged in a political battle.

00:08:37.517 --> 00:08:40.353
They merely wanted
to create the tools

00:08:40.353 --> 00:08:42.288 align:start size:97% position:3%
and the platforms for
other people to communicate

00:08:42.288 --> 00:08:44.657
and I think something changed,

00:08:44.657 --> 00:08:47.226 align:start size:88% position:13%
if I had to sort of
pinpoint when it changed

00:08:47.226 --> 00:08:50.096
it would've been around 2016.

00:08:50.096 --> 00:08:53.165
You know when Trump got elected.

00:08:53.165 --> 00:08:56.969 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think that a lot of big
tech bought into the argument

00:08:56.969 --> 00:09:01.874 align:start size:94% position:6%
that they had sort of
caused Trump to be elected

00:09:01.874 --> 00:09:04.877 align:start size:84% position:16%
or you know, that
sort of disinformation

00:09:04.877 --> 00:09:07.513 align:start size:84% position:16%
had been used through
their platforms

00:09:07.513 --> 00:09:10.349 align:start position:0%
and that that this had resulted
in the election of Trump

00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:12.518
and so therefore they could
no longer just be neutral,

00:09:12.518 --> 00:09:15.121 align:start size:88% position:13%
they would have to take
corrective measures

00:09:15.121 --> 00:09:18.558
to prevent this disinformation

00:09:18.558 --> 00:09:23.229
and I think that was sort of
the beginning of it where again

00:09:23.229 --> 00:09:27.867
the, I think big tech sort
of bought into this idea.

00:09:27.867 --> 00:09:29.669 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think they were
predisposed to buying into it

00:09:29.669 --> 00:09:32.471 align:start size:94% position:6%
because they were liberal
but they were never

00:09:32.471 --> 00:09:35.408
as partisan as they
subsequently became

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:37.143
over the last few years.

00:09:37.143 --> 00:09:40.880
It's not that dissimilar
from I think what happened

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:42.648
with the news industry.

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:44.784 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know there were
people like Brent Bozell,

00:09:44.784 --> 00:09:46.652 line:0%
for years who were doing studies

00:09:46.652 --> 00:09:50.690 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
showing that 90% of
reporters were liberal,

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:52.158 line:0%
voted Democrat and so on.

00:09:52.158 --> 00:09:55.294
But they believed in a
code, a journalistic code

00:09:55.294 --> 00:09:57.463
of objectivity or neutrality.

00:09:57.463 --> 00:09:59.332
And somehow you know over,

00:09:59.332 --> 00:10:01.601 align:start size:94% position:6%
over the last four years,
Trump was perceived

00:10:01.601 --> 00:10:04.136
as such a threat that you know,

00:10:04.136 --> 00:10:06.238
the media decided that
it was more important

00:10:06.238 --> 00:10:08.841
to stop Trump than to
live up to the sort of,

00:10:08.841 --> 00:10:10.476
this code of neutrality

00:10:10.476 --> 00:10:13.613
and I think something similar
happened in big teach as well.

00:10:13.613 --> 00:10:15.247 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah, has it
been tough for you

00:10:15.247 --> 00:10:18.150 align:start size:84% position:16%
as someone that isn't
purely a leftist

00:10:18.150 --> 00:10:20.453 align:start position:0%
regardless of how you fully
defined yourself but you're not,

00:10:20.453 --> 00:10:23.856
you're clearly not
purely leftist but to
be in San Francisco

00:10:23.856 --> 00:10:25.825 align:start size:84% position:16%
and still be around
a lot of that.

00:10:25.825 --> 00:10:27.994 align:start size:88% position:13%
I mean right now we know
