00:14:13.219 --> 00:14:15.421
So yeah, I mean I think that
changes things a little bit.

00:14:15.421 --> 00:14:17.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Yeah all right, so
before we go too far down

00:14:17.389 --> 00:14:18.958
the California thing
and I know you've got

00:14:18.958 --> 00:14:22.161
all kinds of issues
with your DA up there

00:14:22.161 --> 00:14:23.896 align:start size:88% position:13%
and you have challenged
him to a debate,

00:14:23.896 --> 00:14:25.664
we'll see if it actually happens

00:14:25.664 --> 00:14:27.233
but I just thought
we could go into

00:14:27.233 --> 00:14:30.336 align:start size:84% position:16%
some of the basic
stuff about big tech

00:14:30.336 --> 00:14:31.604
that I think people
are confused by.

00:14:31.604 --> 00:14:34.006 align:start size:88% position:13%
So you've written a
bunch about Section 230

00:14:34.006 --> 00:14:35.741 align:start size:94% position:6%
of The Communications Act.
- Yeah.

00:14:35.741 --> 00:14:37.009
- And I thought maybe,

00:14:37.009 --> 00:14:38.310
could you just kind of
clean it up for people

00:14:38.310 --> 00:14:40.312 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I hear a
lot of people saying

00:14:40.312 --> 00:14:43.983
oh Trump should've invoked
230 and now it's too late,

00:14:43.983 --> 00:14:45.317
and we should do it,
we shouldn't do it.

00:14:45.317 --> 00:14:47.720 align:start size:84% position:16%
But basically in
your Medium article,

00:14:47.720 --> 00:14:50.222
you made the argument, it's
a little more nuanced perhaps

00:14:50.222 --> 00:14:51.624 align:start size:84% position:16%
than just invoking it.
- Yeah.

00:14:51.624 --> 00:14:54.260
- Or getting rid of
it or whatever else.

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:57.363 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah, the call that you're
hearing by conservatives

00:14:57.363 --> 00:14:59.765
is that they wanna
repeal Section 230

00:14:59.765 --> 00:15:01.367
which is the liability shield

00:15:01.367 --> 00:15:03.002
that protects big tech companies

00:15:03.002 --> 00:15:05.671 align:start size:88% position:13%
and what that liability
shield does is that

00:15:05.671 --> 00:15:09.875 align:start size:94% position:6%
when somebody posts
content on their platforms

00:15:09.875 --> 00:15:12.745 align:start size:88% position:13%
that would normally
give rise to liability,

00:15:12.745 --> 00:15:14.480
you can only sue the
person who posted it

00:15:14.480 --> 00:15:15.748
and not the platform.

00:15:15.748 --> 00:15:17.483
So it's a little bit
like the distinction

00:15:17.483 --> 00:15:20.920
between a magazine
and a newsstand.

00:15:20.920 --> 00:15:23.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, if the magazine
publishes an article

00:15:23.856 --> 00:15:26.825
that's defamatory, then
the magazine can be sued

00:15:26.825 --> 00:15:29.295 align:start size:94% position:6%
but the newsstand on
which the magazine is sold

00:15:29.295 --> 00:15:30.763
cannot be sued.

00:15:30.763 --> 00:15:32.131 align:start size:97% position:3%
It did not participate in
the creation of that content,

00:15:32.131 --> 00:15:33.666
it merely distributed it.

00:15:33.666 --> 00:15:36.502 align:start size:88% position:13%
That was the distinction
that Section 230

00:15:36.502 --> 00:15:37.736
sought to establish.

00:15:37.736 --> 00:15:39.805
It basically said that
these big tech companies

00:15:39.805 --> 00:15:41.006
and really all tech companies.

00:15:41.006 --> 00:15:42.808 align:start size:84% position:16%
This doesn't just
apply to the big ones

00:15:42.808 --> 00:15:45.377 align:start size:94% position:6%
but to small innovators
as well, they're protected

00:15:45.377 --> 00:15:47.947
as long as all they're
doing is distributing.

00:15:47.947 --> 00:15:50.749
As long as they're not
creating the content

00:15:50.749 --> 00:15:53.185
and I think there's a lot of,

00:15:53.185 --> 00:15:56.989
that makes a lot of sense
because you couldn't have

00:15:56.989 --> 00:15:59.491
restaurant reviews
on Yelp you know,

00:15:59.491 --> 00:16:02.561
if Yelp could be sued
every time you know,

00:16:02.561 --> 00:16:04.530 align:start size:88% position:13%
a small business
didn't like the review.

00:16:04.530 --> 00:16:08.067 align:start size:84% position:16%
You would have, Reddit
was sued recently

00:16:08.067 --> 00:16:10.736 align:start size:88% position:13%
because people in a
comment board lost money

00:16:10.736 --> 00:16:12.738
on one of the trades
that was you know,

00:16:12.738 --> 00:16:14.773 align:start size:81% position:19%
that was posted
in WallStreetBets.

00:16:14.773 --> 00:16:17.977
And so you know,
and so you know,

00:16:17.977 --> 00:16:21.580 align:start size:97% position:3%
Gmail maybe would be imperiled
if Google could be sued

00:16:21.580 --> 00:16:24.516 align:start size:97% position:3%
every time a crime was
committed using Gmail right?

00:16:24.516 --> 00:16:26.352
And so it doesn't make sense

00:16:26.352 --> 00:16:30.289 align:start position:0%
to always hold the tech
platform liable for any content

00:16:30.289 --> 00:16:32.057
that the user is posting.

00:16:32.057 --> 00:16:33.892
You surely go after
those, those users.

00:16:33.892 --> 00:16:36.128
That part of Section
230 which is sort of,

00:16:36.128 --> 00:16:41.000 align:start size:97% position:3%
is provision Section
230(c)(1), that still I think

00:16:41.000 --> 00:16:43.402
makes a lot of sense and if
you were to get rid of it,

00:16:43.402 --> 00:16:46.538
sort of throw the baby
out with the bathwater,

00:16:46.538 --> 00:16:48.307
I think you would hurt a
lot of small innovators.

00:16:48.307 --> 00:16:51.043 align:start size:84% position:16%
There's a lot of
small tech companies

00:16:51.043 --> 00:16:53.412 align:start size:84% position:16%
that rely on that
liability protection

00:16:53.412 --> 00:16:54.913
to offer their service.

00:16:54.913 --> 00:16:59.551 align:start size:97% position:3%
The problem is, it all
came in with Section (c)(2)

00:17:00.986 --> 00:17:03.288
which basically, this is,
it's really interesting.

00:17:03.288 --> 00:17:06.558
This is where Congress,
this is where you know,

00:17:06.558 --> 00:17:08.761 align:start size:94% position:6%
the road to hell is paved
with good intentions.

00:17:08.761 --> 00:17:11.730
What Congress tried to
do in (c)(2) was say

00:17:11.730 --> 00:17:13.265 align:start size:81% position:19%
to the big tech
companies, listen,

00:17:13.265 --> 00:17:15.501
we don't wanna punish you
for being good Samaritans.

00:17:15.501 --> 00:17:18.637 align:start size:94% position:6%
So if you wanna take
down content on your site

00:17:18.637 --> 00:17:23.075 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it's pornographic,
it's obscene,

00:17:23.075 --> 00:17:27.679
it's excessively violent
or otherwise objectionable,

00:17:27.679 --> 00:17:30.716
you can take that down
and we won't hold you

00:17:30.716 --> 00:17:31.950
to be a publisher.

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:34.186
You'll still stay say,
you'll stay a newsstand

00:17:34.186 --> 00:17:38.123
in our eyes and so you
know, Congress tried to,

00:17:38.123 --> 00:17:39.358
Congress was very worried.

00:17:39.358 --> 00:17:42.494
This was 1996 when
the Act was passed,

00:17:42.494 --> 00:17:44.196 align:start size:97% position:3%
it's called the
Communications Decency Act.

00:17:44.196 --> 00:17:47.666
And they were very worried
about smut on the internet

00:17:47.666 --> 00:17:49.535
and they were trying
to give tech companies

00:17:49.535 --> 00:17:52.438 align:start size:84% position:16%
an incentive or
protection to remove

00:17:52.438 --> 00:17:54.273 align:start size:84% position:16%
this type of
objectionable content

00:17:54.273 --> 00:17:56.975 align:start size:88% position:13%
without then triggering
liability and so,

00:17:56.975 --> 00:17:58.977
but see this is where all
the censorship concern

00:17:58.977 --> 00:18:00.112 align:start size:75% position:25%
has now come in.
- Right.

00:18:00.112 --> 00:18:03.048 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Because fastforward
15 years later

00:18:03.048 --> 00:18:06.452
and big tech companies are
using this very broad term,

00:18:06.452 --> 00:18:09.154 align:start size:88% position:13%
otherwise objectionable
to mean content

00:18:09.154 --> 00:18:10.556
that they simply don't like.

00:18:10.556 --> 00:18:12.491 align:start size:94% position:6%
That you know, politically
they don't like it

00:18:12.491 --> 00:18:15.394 align:start size:88% position:13%
and so they now have all
this censorship power

00:18:15.394 --> 00:18:17.362 align:start size:88% position:13%
and this is where all
the problem has come in.

00:18:17.362 --> 00:18:19.364 align:start size:84% position:16%
This is why you know,
Trump and others

00:18:19.364 --> 00:18:21.500 align:start size:84% position:16%
have called for the
repeal of Section 230.

00:18:21.500 --> 00:18:25.504
I understand that, the desire.

00:18:25.504 --> 00:18:28.340
Because I think the
censorship is wrong

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:29.842
but I just think that we need a,

00:18:29.842 --> 00:18:31.376
we need a slightly
different solution.

00:18:31.376 --> 00:18:34.513
- Right, so do you have
a sense of what goes on

00:18:34.513 --> 00:18:36.782
at the board meetings
of these companies

00:18:36.782 --> 00:18:38.717
when they're trying to
make these policies?

00:18:38.717 --> 00:18:40.285
Because I think a
lot of people think

00:18:40.285 --> 00:18:42.588
and I've tried to get my
audience off this idea

00:18:42.588 --> 00:18:44.089
that it's a couple
of guys sitting there

00:18:44.089 --> 00:18:45.891
and they all hate conservatives

00:18:45.891 --> 00:18:47.993
and the goal is just to
destroy conservatives

00:18:47.993 --> 00:18:49.294 align:start size:84% position:16%
'cause I don't think
it's that simple.

00:18:49.294 --> 00:18:51.330 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think it's much
more complex than that

00:18:51.330 --> 00:18:54.199 align:start size:84% position:16%
but what would be
like a sensible policy

00:18:54.199 --> 00:18:55.701
'cause it seems fairly obvious.

00:18:55.701 --> 00:18:58.403
You can see it in
Twitter trends.

00:18:58.403 --> 00:19:00.038 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know I screencapture
them all the time

00:19:00.038 --> 00:19:01.740
and it's very obvious when,

00:19:01.740 --> 00:19:03.976
sometimes they're
actually trying

00:19:03.976 --> 00:19:06.145
to push a mob towards somebody.

00:19:07.312 --> 00:19:09.448
So what would be
a sensible policy

00:19:09.448 --> 00:19:14.453 align:start size:97% position:3%
that you think would be
honest and legal I suppose?

00:19:15.888 --> 00:19:18.090
- Well I'm pretty happy with
the First Amendment you know?

00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:20.826 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think the First Amendment
and then 230 years

00:19:20.826 --> 00:19:23.829 align:start size:97% position:3%
of Supreme Court case law
provides us with the standard

00:19:23.829 --> 00:19:26.165
and that the Supreme
Court for two centuries

00:19:26.165 --> 00:19:30.068
has been wrestling with the
problem of dangerous speech.

00:19:30.068 --> 00:19:33.405 align:start position:0%
I think there's a misconception
that the First Amendment

00:19:33.405 --> 00:19:35.807
allows anything
goes, it doesn't.

00:19:35.807 --> 00:19:38.610
There are at least nine
categories of speech

00:19:38.610 --> 00:19:40.812
that are not protected
by the First Amendment

00:19:40.812 --> 00:19:42.047
because they're dangerous.

00:19:42.047 --> 00:19:44.983
You cannot yell fire
in a crowded theater,

00:19:44.983 --> 00:19:47.986 align:start size:88% position:13%
you cannot incite people
to commit a crime.

00:19:47.986 --> 00:19:50.088
You can't engage in fraud.

00:19:50.088 --> 00:19:52.791
You know you can't, you
can't defame somebody.

00:19:52.791 --> 00:19:55.227
You know fighting
words are illegal.

00:19:55.227 --> 00:19:57.496
So there's a lot of
categories of speech

00:19:57.496 --> 00:20:00.332
that aren't protected
and most of the speech

00:20:00.332 --> 00:20:03.302 align:start size:97% position:3%
that causes social networks
the greatest problems,

00:20:03.302 --> 00:20:05.304
they fall into one
of those categories

00:20:05.304 --> 00:20:08.507 align:start size:84% position:16%
and so I think it
would be very doable

00:20:08.507 --> 00:20:11.577 align:start size:94% position:6%
for them to create a
content moderation policy

00:20:11.577 --> 00:20:15.080
that is broadly consistent
with the First Amendment.

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:18.050
Now the reason why I say
broadly is because look,

00:20:18.050 --> 00:20:20.018
take defamation for example.

00:20:20.018 --> 00:20:22.521
We're not gonna do a
court trial every time

00:20:22.521 --> 00:20:24.423 align:start size:94% position:6%
that a user complains that
they're being harassed

00:20:24.423 --> 00:20:26.158 align:start size:84% position:16%
by another user okay?
- Right.

00:20:26.158 --> 00:20:27.593
- But what we're gonna do is,

00:20:27.593 --> 00:20:29.328 align:start size:88% position:13%
we're gonna have a
standard that says that

00:20:29.328 --> 00:20:32.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
if a user complains
about another user's post

00:20:32.864 --> 00:20:36.969
and that post is
facially defamatory

00:20:36.969 --> 00:20:38.537 align:start size:88% position:13%
about that other user
we're gonna take it down

00:20:38.537 --> 00:20:41.807
because there are billions
of these social media posts

00:20:41.807 --> 00:20:44.243 align:start size:88% position:13%
and we're not gonna
run a trial every time.

00:20:44.243 --> 00:20:47.179
But, we're gonna
take our guidelines

00:20:47.179 --> 00:20:49.081
from First Amendment case law

00:20:49.081 --> 00:20:51.783 align:start size:97% position:3%
and those are basically gonna
be the broad categories.

00:20:51.783 --> 00:20:55.787
What we're not gonna do
is create new categories

00:20:55.787 --> 00:21:00.692
of speech that are prohibited
because that would appropriate

00:21:02.094 --> 00:21:06.398
to the big tech companies,
extraordinary power you know?

00:21:06.398 --> 00:21:08.100
- But do you think the problem,

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:11.903 align:start position:0%
do you think the inherent
problem is that because so much

00:21:11.903 --> 00:21:14.172
of the social justice stuff

00:21:14.172 --> 00:21:17.142
has now leaked into these
companies that you know,

00:21:17.142 --> 00:21:18.610
I can get on board
what you just said.

00:21:18.610 --> 00:21:19.845
Most of my audience
can get on board

00:21:19.845 --> 00:21:22.247
with what you just
said but they can't

00:21:22.247 --> 00:21:25.350 align:start size:84% position:16%
because they've
accepted this ideology

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:28.587 align:start size:97% position:3%
that many ways is just
completely counter to that.

00:21:28.587 --> 00:21:31.523
- I think ideology
is very motivating

00:21:31.523 --> 00:21:33.925
especially for a lot of
the low level employees

00:21:33.925 --> 00:21:35.961
who've banded together
and created pressure

00:21:35.961 --> 00:21:38.764 align:start size:97% position:3%
on the leadership, the
management of these companies.

00:21:38.764 --> 00:21:42.467 align:start size:94% position:6%
They've created these
campaigns, these boycotts.

00:21:42.467 --> 00:21:44.870
There's also pressure
coming from above,

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:46.772 align:start size:84% position:16%
from the Senate
Judiciary Committee

00:21:46.772 --> 00:21:49.941 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that Committee
basically, in these hearings,

00:21:49.941 --> 00:21:54.112 align:start position:0%
the people are now, the leading
members of that committee

00:21:55.280 --> 00:21:58.183 line:0%
keep, basically are
telling big tech

00:21:58.183 --> 00:22:00.052 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
on the one hand we
want you to censor more

00:22:00.052 --> 00:22:03.221 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
and on the other, we're
thinking about breaking you up

00:22:03.221 --> 00:22:07.025
and so there's a lot
of pressure I think

00:22:07.025 --> 00:22:09.861
that big tech is feeling
that if they don't go along

00:22:09.861 --> 00:22:13.365 align:start size:97% position:3%
with these policies that
either they're gonna you know,

00:22:13.365 --> 00:22:18.203
anger the people above and
that may increase the chances

00:22:18.203 --> 00:22:19.905
they get busted up.

00:22:19.905 --> 00:22:21.406
Or that they will, you know,

00:22:21.406 --> 00:22:22.808
they're gonna upset
sort of this pressure

00:22:22.808 --> 00:22:25.277 align:start size:88% position:13%
that's coming from below
from the employees.

00:22:25.277 --> 00:22:27.012
I don't think it's a very
good excuse by the way,

00:22:27.012 --> 00:22:28.347
I think they should have

00:22:28.347 --> 00:22:30.215
more courage and
backbone than that.

00:22:30.215 --> 00:22:32.918 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think our commitment
to free speech

00:22:32.918 --> 00:22:35.354 align:start size:88% position:13%
should be made of
sterner stuff than that.

00:22:35.354 --> 00:22:36.521 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Yeah.
- But I think the reality is

00:22:36.521 --> 00:22:37.923 align:start size:84% position:16%
that these companies
are you know,

00:22:37.923 --> 00:22:40.425 align:start size:84% position:16%
succumbing to
pressure campaigns.

00:22:40.425 --> 00:22:41.860
- What do you think just broadly

00:22:41.860 --> 00:22:44.062 align:start size:97% position:3%
about just the amount of
power that they have over us?

00:22:44.062 --> 00:22:46.198
Because you mentioned the
First Amendment before,

00:22:46.198 --> 00:22:48.834 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't think the founders
could've ever imagined

00:22:48.834 --> 00:22:50.969 align:start size:94% position:6%
that there would basically
be four companies

00:22:50.969 --> 00:22:52.671
that in many ways I
think you could argue

00:22:52.671 --> 00:22:54.373 align:start size:84% position:16%
are more powerful
than the government.

00:22:54.373 --> 00:22:56.908
At least in our day to
day lives at this point.

00:22:56.908 --> 00:22:59.745
What do we do about
that amount of power?

00:22:59.745 --> 00:23:01.613 align:start size:97% position:3%
- They do have an
extraordinary amount of power

00:23:01.613 --> 00:23:04.950
because what happened is the
town square got privatized.

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:06.518
Free speech got digitized,

00:23:06.518 --> 00:23:08.854 align:start size:88% position:13%
that caused the town
square to get privatized

00:23:08.854 --> 00:23:10.922 align:start size:88% position:13%
and the First Amendment
got euthanized.

00:23:10.922 --> 00:23:13.592
You know, because you know,

00:23:13.592 --> 00:23:15.961
when the framers
of the Constitution

00:23:15.961 --> 00:23:17.129
wrote the First Amendment,

00:23:17.129 --> 00:23:18.597
where did you go
for free speech?

00:23:18.597 --> 00:23:20.399 align:start size:94% position:6%
There were a multiplicity
of town squares

00:23:20.399 --> 00:23:22.501 align:start size:97% position:3%
all over the country, there
were thousands of them.

00:23:22.501 --> 00:23:23.969 align:start size:84% position:16%
You could get on the
courthouse steps,

00:23:23.969 --> 00:23:25.404
pull out your soapbox.

00:23:25.404 --> 00:23:26.671 align:start size:94% position:6%
You could speak and anyone
who wanted to listen

00:23:26.671 --> 00:23:27.939
could gather around.

00:23:27.939 --> 00:23:30.208
That's why the First
Amendment contains

00:23:30.208 --> 00:23:31.410 align:start size:81% position:19%
not just the right
to free speech

00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:33.712 align:start size:84% position:16%
but a right to
peacefully assemble.

00:23:33.712 --> 00:23:35.480 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well where do people
assemble today?

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:37.015
On these massive social networks

00:23:37.015 --> 00:23:38.717
which have gigantic
network effects.

00:23:38.717 --> 00:23:41.119
That is where you go to be heard

00:23:41.119 --> 00:23:42.621
and if you cannot
express yourself

00:23:42.621 --> 00:23:44.089
on one of these social networks,

00:23:44.089 --> 00:23:45.624
to what extent do you still
have a free speech right

00:23:45.624 --> 00:23:46.892
in this country?

00:23:46.892 --> 00:23:49.227
I don't think you
do and what we saw

00:23:49.227 --> 00:23:51.863
and what we were seeing you
know, since the election

00:23:51.863 --> 00:23:54.166 align:start size:88% position:13%
is that you effectively
have a cartel

00:23:54.166 --> 00:23:56.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
of these big tech
companies coming together,

00:23:56.968 --> 00:23:58.970 align:start size:84% position:16%
making identical
decisions, deny people

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:00.338
their free speech rights.

00:24:00.338 --> 00:24:01.673
And you know, again,

00:24:01.673 --> 00:24:03.542
to what extent do
you really have

00:24:03.542 --> 00:24:05.444 align:start size:84% position:16%
a First Amendment
right in this country

00:24:05.444 --> 00:24:07.078
when the town square
has been privatized

00:24:07.078 --> 00:24:09.281
and you've been deplatformed.

00:24:09.281 --> 00:24:10.782
- Can you talk a little
bit about how you think

00:24:10.782 --> 00:24:12.017
that cartel works?

00:24:12.017 --> 00:24:13.852
'Cause you've written a
little bit about this.

00:24:13.852 --> 00:24:16.922
Like when Trump got taken
out after January 6th,

00:24:16.922 --> 00:24:19.624 align:start size:97% position:3%
when they took him off Spotify
and Pinterest and Twitter

00:24:19.624 --> 00:24:21.193
and YouTube.

00:24:21.193 --> 00:24:22.661
You know, it wasn't even
the ones where he was.

00:24:22.661 --> 00:24:24.162 align:start position:0%
It's like okay now he can't
listen to music anymore either.

00:24:24.162 --> 00:24:26.097
We're just gonna take
him out of everything.

00:24:26.097 --> 00:24:29.501 align:start size:84% position:16%
That they operate
sort of as a cartel.

00:24:29.501 --> 00:24:31.736 align:start size:81% position:19%
They just wait for
the first mover

00:24:31.736 --> 00:24:33.572
and then everybody
sort of in a minute

00:24:33.572 --> 00:24:35.006
does the exact same thing.

00:24:35.006 --> 00:24:37.509 align:start size:84% position:16%
Well so Jack Dorsey
actually explained it.

00:24:37.509 --> 00:24:39.711 align:start size:84% position:16%
He wrote a Tweet storm
in which he needed,

00:24:39.711 --> 00:24:42.280
felt the need to
defend their action

00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:45.317
to permanently deplatform Trump.

00:24:45.317 --> 00:24:46.985
- Well you had a funny,
you had a funny comment

00:24:46.985 --> 00:24:48.220
on what he said.

00:24:48.220 --> 00:24:49.488
Because it's like,
he does these things

00:24:49.488 --> 00:24:51.056
and then he suddenly
feels very emotional

00:24:51.056 --> 00:24:54.192
about what he's done and then
he opens up about everything.

00:24:54.192 --> 00:24:55.527
- They make these
kneejerk decisions

00:24:55.527 --> 00:24:57.162
that are just like a
violent reaction to it

00:24:57.162 --> 00:24:58.697
and then because they don't,

00:24:58.697 --> 00:25:00.732
they have a blindspot with
respect to their you know,

00:25:00.732 --> 00:25:03.368
their partisan biases
and then they realize,

00:25:03.368 --> 00:25:05.070
oh wait a second we
just did something

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:07.539
that is a big deal
and then he comes out

00:25:07.539 --> 00:25:09.841 align:start position:0%
and then gets very introspective
and tries to explain it.

00:25:09.841 --> 00:25:11.576
At least he tries to explain it,

00:25:11.576 --> 00:25:14.513
that makes Jack my
favorite oligarch.

00:25:14.513 --> 00:25:19.518 align:start size:84% position:16%
But what he said is
that when we, Twitter,

00:25:20.252 --> 00:25:22.787
decided to ban Trump.

00:25:22.787 --> 00:25:25.423
We didn't think it
was that big a deal

00:25:25.423 --> 00:25:28.059 align:start size:88% position:13%
because there were
other places he could go

00:25:28.059 --> 00:25:30.295
to basically get his
free speech right.

00:25:30.295 --> 00:25:33.498 align:start size:97% position:3%
And then all these other
companies did the same thing.

00:25:33.498 --> 00:25:37.102
And he's right, if
only one company

00:25:37.102 --> 00:25:39.538
were to you know, deplatform you

00:25:39.538 --> 00:25:41.206
it might not be that big a deal

00:25:41.206 --> 00:25:43.208
but when all of them are
doing it at the same time,

00:25:43.208 --> 00:25:45.610 align:start size:97% position:3%
they're acting as
effectively a speech cartel.

00:25:45.610 --> 00:25:48.914
Now what Jack said is that they,

00:25:48.914 --> 00:25:51.383
that all of us, you
know didn't collude

00:25:51.383 --> 00:25:54.819
to make these decisions but
we emboldened each other.

00:25:54.819 --> 00:25:55.854
That was his word is embolden.

00:25:55.854 --> 00:25:58.323
It sounds a lot to me,

00:25:58.323 --> 00:26:01.259 align:start size:97% position:3%
what we would call signaling
in the anti-trust law

00:26:01.259 --> 00:26:02.961
where you have a
bunch of companies

00:26:02.961 --> 00:26:05.330 align:start size:84% position:16%
that normally compete
with each other

00:26:05.330 --> 00:26:08.066
and therefore are trying to
make different decisions,

00:26:08.066 --> 00:26:09.968
not all trying to get
to the same decision.

00:26:09.968 --> 00:26:11.670 align:start size:88% position:13%
They're trying to
compete with each other,

00:26:11.670 --> 00:26:13.972
but signaling occurs when
one company does something

00:26:13.972 --> 00:26:16.441
and then you know, all the
other companies follow suit.

00:26:16.441 --> 00:26:20.378 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that's basically
what's happening is that,

00:26:20.378 --> 00:26:23.481
is that with each
incremental company

00:26:23.481 --> 00:26:25.750
that decides to ban
Trump or whoever,

00:26:25.750 --> 00:26:28.453
the pressure builds on
all the rest of them

00:26:28.453 --> 00:26:29.721
to do the same thing.

00:26:29.721 --> 00:26:32.290
Otherwise they'll be
subject to you know,

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:33.391 align:start size:81% position:19%
letterwritting
campaigns and boy,

00:26:33.391 --> 00:26:35.126
and all that kind of stuff.

00:26:35.126 --> 00:26:37.929 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so, you know,
whether they're actively
colluding or not

00:26:37.929 --> 00:26:39.564 align:start size:84% position:16%
or whether it's merely
just the signaling,

00:26:39.564 --> 00:26:43.268 align:start size:88% position:13%
you have the same effect
which is a cartel.

00:26:43.268 --> 00:26:44.903
- So would you break them up?

00:26:44.903 --> 00:26:47.439
I mean would you use
the force of government

00:26:47.439 --> 00:26:49.941
to break up some of these
companies at this point?

00:26:49.941 --> 00:26:51.643
- I would seriously look at it

00:26:51.643 --> 00:26:53.111 align:start size:84% position:16%
because I think they
are too powerful

00:26:53.111 --> 00:26:54.980 align:start size:97% position:3%
but I don't think it's gonna
solve the speech problem

00:26:54.980 --> 00:26:56.448
for this reason.

00:26:56.448 --> 00:26:59.250
Imagine that Google
is busted up into,

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:00.852 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know we have
kind of Google Search

00:27:00.852 --> 00:27:03.288 align:start size:88% position:13%
and then YouTube becomes
its own property.

00:27:03.288 --> 00:27:05.557
You know, maybe there's
like a third division

00:27:05.557 --> 00:27:07.459
for enterprise.

00:27:07.459 --> 00:27:10.328 align:start size:97% position:3%
Well I mean YouTube is the one
that we sort of care about.

00:27:10.328 --> 00:27:12.831 align:start size:88% position:13%
It's, we're not gonna
divide that property up.

00:27:12.831 --> 00:27:14.065
It's still gonna be run.

00:27:14.065 --> 00:27:15.367 align:start size:84% position:16%
It'll just have a
different cap table,

00:27:15.367 --> 00:27:17.802
it's gonna have the
same exact gives.

00:27:17.802 --> 00:27:20.972
They all drink from the same
sort of monocultural fountain,

00:27:20.972 --> 00:27:24.275 align:start size:88% position:13%
they all have the
same ideological
commitments and biases.

00:27:24.275 --> 00:27:27.412
They're subject to most
of the same pressures.

00:27:27.412 --> 00:27:30.615
So I don't know that if
you effectively increase

00:27:30.615 --> 00:27:35.020
the size of speech cartel from
five members, to 10 members.

00:27:35.020 --> 00:27:37.322
I don't know that that
by itself does anything.

00:27:37.322 --> 00:27:41.059 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think what we need and I'm
not expecting to get this

00:27:41.059 --> 00:27:43.495
from Congress any time soon is,

00:27:43.495 --> 00:27:47.632
some sort of common
carrier obligations

00:27:47.632 --> 00:27:50.735 align:start size:84% position:16%
imposed on these
big tech monopolies.

00:27:50.735 --> 00:27:53.138
This is a proposal that
came from Richard Epstein

00:27:53.138 --> 00:27:56.241
who's a Professor at the
University of Chicago.

00:27:56.241 --> 00:27:57.642
Basically what he says is look,

00:27:57.642 --> 00:27:59.978 align:start size:97% position:3%
if you're a gigantic
monopoly, you're a utility.

00:27:59.978 --> 00:28:04.883 align:start size:97% position:3%
You can't deny a service to
somebody based on their creed.

00:28:04.883 --> 00:28:09.054
You know that's the
non-discrimination
rule that we need.

00:28:09.054 --> 00:28:12.724
Imagine if you know, like
a common carrier would be

00:28:12.724 --> 00:28:14.693
something like a railroad.

00:28:14.693 --> 00:28:17.462
Where it's a natural monopoly.

00:28:17.462 --> 00:28:20.565 align:start size:97% position:3%
Imagine if in the days of
the Lincoln-Douglas debates,

00:28:20.565 --> 00:28:22.734 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know they got around
the country by train.

00:28:22.734 --> 00:28:25.770
Imagine if I don't
know, some oligarch,

00:28:25.770 --> 00:28:27.272
Cornelius Vanderbilt
or something said,

00:28:27.272 --> 00:28:31.009 align:start size:97% position:3%
you know Mr. Lincoln, I
don't like your point of view

00:28:31.009 --> 00:28:31.943
in this debate.

00:28:31.943 --> 00:28:33.812
I'm gonna deny you service,

00:28:33.812 --> 00:28:35.313 align:start size:97% position:3%
I don't want you to be
able to express your views.

00:28:35.313 --> 00:28:38.283
Well there's no way that we
would've let that happen.

00:28:38.283 --> 00:28:41.619
We wouldn't let somebody,
we wouldn't let monopolies

00:28:41.619 --> 00:28:44.122
be that powerful where
they can deny service

00:28:44.122 --> 00:28:47.692
to essential facilities to
people based on their creed

00:28:47.692 --> 00:28:51.496 align:start size:97% position:3%
and that basically is the
legislative fix here I think.

00:28:51.496 --> 00:28:55.667
More than doing something
like repealing Section 230

00:28:55.667 --> 00:28:57.502
which I think would hurt
a lot of small innovators.

00:28:57.502 --> 00:28:59.671
- Right.
- So yeah,

00:28:59.671 --> 00:29:01.573
but look you know, I'm
not expecting to get that

00:29:01.573 --> 00:29:04.109
in the next two years
or even four years, so.

00:29:04.109 --> 00:29:05.410 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I'm guessing generally
speaking though

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:07.712 align:start position:0%
you'd rather have
technological solutions to this

00:29:07.712 --> 00:29:09.147
than governmental solutions.

00:29:10.248 --> 00:29:12.383
I would but, but I don't know

00:29:12.383 --> 00:29:14.119
that we're gonna get a
technological solution

00:29:14.119 --> 00:29:18.056
because I do think that you
know, monopolies are real.

00:29:18.056 --> 00:29:19.457
Part of what I do as an investor

00:29:19.457 --> 00:29:21.893
is try and invest in the
next set of monopolies

00:29:21.893 --> 00:29:26.898 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you know, you know that's
what Peter's whole book.

00:29:27.832 --> 00:29:29.000 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah.
- "Zero to One" is about

00:29:29.000 --> 00:29:30.568 align:start size:84% position:16%
is there's two
kinds of companies.

00:29:30.568 --> 00:29:31.803
- Right there,

00:29:31.803 --> 00:29:33.304
it's right there.
- On the shelf.

00:29:33.304 --> 00:29:34.539
Yeah, I mean there's two
kinds of companies right?

00:29:34.539 --> 00:29:36.674
There's monopolies
and commodities.

00:29:36.674 --> 00:29:37.876
You wanna be a monopoly.

00:29:37.876 --> 00:29:39.110 align:start size:88% position:13%
Well monopolies have
incredible market power.

00:29:39.110 --> 00:29:43.915
I think it was fine
when those monopolies

00:29:43.915 --> 00:29:47.352 align:start position:0%
were lawfully gained and weren't
trying to use their power

00:29:47.352 --> 00:29:51.089
to put their thumb on the
scale of American democracy

00:29:51.089 --> 00:29:53.725 align:start size:84% position:16%
and you know it
wasn't that long ago

00:29:53.725 --> 00:29:58.396
that these big tech
companies would state

00:29:58.396 --> 00:30:00.698
their commitment to neutrality.

00:30:00.698 --> 00:30:02.167
I mean Mark Zuckerberg gave,

00:30:02.167 --> 00:30:06.371 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think a very good speech
just a few years ago, in 2018.

00:30:06.371 --> 00:30:10.108
In which he said that,
he said social networks

00:30:10.108 --> 00:30:11.509
were the fifth estate.

00:30:11.509 --> 00:30:14.179 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know that the traditional
media historically

00:30:14.179 --> 00:30:15.680
was the fourth estate.

00:30:15.680 --> 00:30:17.949 line:0%
I think one through
three were like,

00:30:17.949 --> 00:30:20.018 line:0%
I don't know they go back
to Europe or something

00:30:20.018 --> 00:30:21.286 line:0%
like the nobility
and the peasants

00:30:21.286 --> 00:30:22.620 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
or something.
- It's the people, yeah yeah.

00:30:22.620 --> 00:30:23.855 line:0%
- The people yeah yeah yeah.

00:30:23.855 --> 00:30:25.824 align:start size:88% position:13%
Anyway the fourth estate
is traditional media.

00:30:25.824 --> 00:30:27.692
What Zuckerberg said
is that social networks

00:30:27.692 --> 00:30:30.662
were the fifth estate and
that they posed a threat

00:30:30.662 --> 00:30:33.331
to the people in power just
like the fourth estate did

00:30:33.331 --> 00:30:36.000 align:start size:88% position:13%
and therefore it
needed to be protected.

00:30:36.000 --> 00:30:40.605
And I think he's right
about that and they,

00:30:40.605 --> 00:30:43.374
the sad thing is that
he has felt the need

00:30:43.374 --> 00:30:46.077
to kind of move
off that position

00:30:46.077 --> 00:30:49.981
and engage in this
sort of censorship.

00:30:49.981 --> 00:30:51.516
He's, you know.

00:30:51.516 --> 00:30:54.352 align:start size:97% position:3%
- When you see these guys have
to go in front of Congress,

00:30:54.352 --> 00:30:56.354
when you see Jack or Zuckerberg.

00:30:56.354 --> 00:30:58.223
I don't know if you
know them personally,

00:30:58.223 --> 00:31:00.258
you know probably at least
somewhere in the circles

00:31:00.258 --> 00:31:01.759
they're somewhat close.

00:31:01.759 --> 00:31:04.195 align:start size:94% position:6%
But when you see them have
to basically be arguing

00:31:04.195 --> 00:31:06.464
with people who have
so little understanding

00:31:06.464 --> 00:31:08.600
of what the real issues
are, any of that stuff.

00:31:08.600 --> 00:31:12.604
Does it just like drive
you crazy to see it?

00:31:12.604 --> 00:31:14.939
- It's a little
painful you know,

00:31:14.939 --> 00:31:18.676 align:start size:97% position:3%
because the people doing
the questioning by and large

00:31:18.676 --> 00:31:20.945
don't have the knowledge to,

00:31:20.945 --> 00:31:24.849
or ability to kind of
interrogate these CEOs

00:31:24.849 --> 00:31:27.118 align:start size:88% position:13%
the way that they should
but some of them do.

00:31:27.118 --> 00:31:29.487
You know there have been
some interesting moments

00:31:29.487 --> 00:31:33.558 align:start size:97% position:3%
where these CEOs get questions
and sort of dressed down.

00:31:33.558 --> 00:31:34.792
I mean there's a lot of theater,

00:31:34.792 --> 00:31:35.860
it's very performative.

00:31:35.860 --> 00:31:37.962
There's a lot of grandstanding.

00:31:37.962 --> 00:31:39.864
I mean, I don't really
know how much progress

00:31:39.864 --> 00:31:41.666
gets made in these hearings.

00:31:41.666 --> 00:31:44.035
But the point of
it I think is valid

00:31:44.035 --> 00:31:47.005
which is you have
these, a handful

00:31:47.005 --> 00:31:50.975
of tech oligarchs who now
control the public square

00:31:50.975 --> 00:31:54.178 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they have
appropriated to themselves

00:31:54.178 --> 00:31:57.615 align:start size:84% position:16%
a vast power to
decide who has access

00:31:57.615 --> 00:31:59.083
and which views have access

00:31:59.083 --> 00:32:02.120
and which views they're
gonna silence and censor

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:04.222
and basically ghost.

00:32:04.222 --> 00:32:07.091 align:start position:0%
And you know, and no one
elected them to have that power

00:32:07.091 --> 00:32:09.227 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think we should be
rightly concerned about that

00:32:09.227 --> 00:32:11.829
and I think we should
question them about

00:32:11.829 --> 00:32:13.965 align:start size:84% position:16%
how they are
wielding that power.

00:32:13.965 --> 00:32:16.134 align:start size:97% position:3%
- How much of an escalation
do you think it was

00:32:16.134 --> 00:32:19.604
when Parler got nuked by Amazon?

00:32:19.604 --> 00:32:21.406 align:start size:84% position:16%
Because that was
completely different,

00:32:21.406 --> 00:32:23.641 align:start size:84% position:16%
taking away servers
than you know,

00:32:23.641 --> 00:32:26.077
taking away speech of
an individual person.

00:32:27.278 --> 00:32:28.513
- Yeah, I mean it
was an escalation

00:32:28.513 --> 00:32:32.050 align:start size:88% position:13%
because you had not just
you know, one person,

00:32:32.050 --> 00:32:36.521 align:start size:97% position:3%
Trump or his supporters
being ghosted or deplatformed

00:32:36.521 --> 00:32:40.158 align:start size:97% position:3%
but you had 16 million Parler
users or whatever it is.

00:32:40.158 --> 00:32:42.894
99.9% of whom never
did anything wrong.

00:32:42.894 --> 00:32:44.929
You know and that
was a diminishment

00:32:44.929 --> 00:32:46.564
of their free speech rights.

00:32:46.564 --> 00:32:50.468
It was also I think unjustified,

00:32:50.468 --> 00:32:55.440 align:start size:97% position:3%
if the justification for
that was that Parler was used

00:32:56.674 --> 00:32:59.944
in the planning of the
events of January 6th,

00:32:59.944 --> 00:33:03.881 align:start size:97% position:3%
it was clearly a scapegoating
because we now have,

00:33:03.881 --> 00:33:05.283
FBI charging documents

00:33:05.283 --> 00:33:06.217 align:start size:72% position:28%
have come out.
- Yeah.

00:33:06.217 --> 00:33:08.686
- They've filed, 226 cases.

00:33:08.686 --> 00:33:11.756 align:start size:94% position:6%
The social networking site
that was used 10x more

00:33:11.756 --> 00:33:13.391 align:start size:84% position:16%
than all the others
was Facebook.

00:33:13.391 --> 00:33:15.626
You know Parler was used
one-tenth of the amount

00:33:15.626 --> 00:33:18.730
that Facebook was but
Parler was you know,

00:33:18.730 --> 00:33:20.898 align:start size:84% position:16%
was sort of an
easy, an easy target

00:33:20.898 --> 00:33:22.166
to sort of scapegoat for that.

00:33:22.166 --> 00:33:23.735
- Is that purely because of,

00:33:23.735 --> 00:33:25.603
the sort of the cartel mindset

00:33:25.603 --> 00:33:27.572
that you were discussing before?

00:33:27.572 --> 00:33:29.374 align:start size:97% position:3%
Like there's certain,
there's however many people

00:33:29.374 --> 00:33:31.142 align:start size:84% position:16%
or companies are
involved in the cartel

00:33:31.142 --> 00:33:32.343
and here's just this new guy.

00:33:32.343 --> 00:33:34.345
So it's like, well
they are competition,

00:33:34.345 --> 00:33:36.114
there are 21 million
people over there.

00:33:36.114 --> 00:33:38.282 align:start size:84% position:16%
May as well just
take them out right?

00:33:38.282 --> 00:33:42.520
- Yeah, I mean it's, yeah I
think they're an easy target.

00:33:42.520 --> 00:33:45.623
I think Facebook clearly
was eager to deflect blame

00:33:45.623 --> 00:33:46.891
onto Parler.

00:33:46.891 --> 00:33:48.126
There was actually a
pretty good article

00:33:48.126 --> 00:33:50.395
in The Washington
Post about that.

00:33:50.395 --> 00:33:53.231 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know calling
Facebook out on that,

00:33:53.231 --> 00:33:55.533
pointing to the fact that
they had a lot more of this

00:33:55.533 --> 00:33:58.970
problematic content
than Parler did.

00:33:58.970 --> 00:34:00.772
But yeah I mean it's an easy way

00:34:00.772 --> 00:34:02.173
for these big tech
companies to show

00:34:02.173 --> 00:34:05.877 align:start size:97% position:3%
that they're doing something
to control you know,

00:34:05.877 --> 00:34:09.781 align:start size:94% position:6%
disinformation or whatever
the powers that be now,

00:34:09.781 --> 00:34:11.315
you know are concerned about.

00:34:11.315 --> 00:34:14.118
Again I think this all goes
back to the fact that these,

00:34:14.118 --> 00:34:15.987 align:start size:84% position:16%
the big tech companies
are very worried

00:34:15.987 --> 00:34:17.221 align:start size:84% position:16%
about getting
broken up right now.

00:34:17.221 --> 00:34:18.756
They don't wanna get busted up

00:34:18.756 --> 00:34:21.225
and so they're looking to
curry favor with you know,

00:34:21.225 --> 00:34:23.728 align:start size:94% position:6%
the current administration
in Washington.

00:34:23.728 --> 00:34:25.930
- So I can hear all my
pure libertarian fans

00:34:25.930 --> 00:34:27.398
in the back of my
head going okay,

00:34:27.398 --> 00:34:29.434 align:start size:88% position:13%
you guys are talking too
much about government

00:34:29.434 --> 00:34:31.135
and antitrust and regulation

00:34:31.135 --> 00:34:32.937
and breaking them up
and all that stuff

00:34:32.937 --> 00:34:35.506 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you should be talking
about decentralization

00:34:35.506 --> 00:34:37.508
and crypto and everything else.

00:34:37.508 --> 00:34:41.012
Do you think there are
enough answers there?

00:34:41.012 --> 00:34:43.314
Basically to solve
a lot of this stuff.

00:34:44.415 --> 00:34:46.084
- Not in the nearterm.

00:34:46.084 --> 00:34:48.386
I mean, I'm a fan of a lot
of those crypto projects

00:34:48.386 --> 00:34:51.389
but they are very much projects.

00:34:51.389 --> 00:34:53.825
I would like to be able
to call them companies,

00:34:53.825 --> 00:34:55.159 align:start size:84% position:16%
some of them I
guess are companies

00:34:55.159 --> 00:34:57.562
but they're still at
a super early stage.

00:34:57.562 --> 00:35:00.731 align:start size:97% position:3%
You know, we're still building
out poor infrastructure

00:35:00.731 --> 00:35:03.835
of these blockchain platforms.

00:35:03.835 --> 00:35:07.638
To my knowledge there has not
been a breakout application

00:35:07.638 --> 00:35:11.109 align:start size:97% position:3%
built on top of any of these
sort of crypto platforms

00:35:11.109 --> 00:35:13.244
except if you wanna you know,

00:35:13.244 --> 00:35:17.415 align:start size:97% position:3%
except for Bitcoin and then
DeFi or decentralized finance.

00:35:17.415 --> 00:35:19.550
Basically financial speculation.

00:35:19.550 --> 00:35:23.988
I think we're a long
way off from you know,

00:35:23.988 --> 00:35:27.558 align:start size:84% position:16%
being able to run
truly decentralized,

00:35:27.558 --> 00:35:31.462
say speech apps with
billions of users.

00:35:31.462 --> 00:35:36.300
You know that seems to me,
years off if not decades

00:35:36.300 --> 00:35:39.637
and I'm not really willing
to kind of wait that long

00:35:39.637 --> 00:35:42.840
to kind of have the
First Amendment back.

00:35:42.840 --> 00:35:44.342
- Yeah and by the way,

00:35:44.342 --> 00:35:45.643
that creates a whole host
of other problems right

00:35:45.643 --> 00:35:47.078
because then you
could put all sorts

00:35:47.078 --> 00:35:49.347 align:start size:94% position:6%
of horrible stuff on
there and once it's there,

00:35:49.347 --> 00:35:51.682
it's there and then
you got a whole bunch

00:35:51.682 --> 00:35:53.117
of other problems.

00:35:53.117 --> 00:35:55.853
- Yeah it's not clear exact,

00:35:55.853 --> 00:35:58.089 align:start size:97% position:3%
I mean yeah you need to
have some sort of regulation

00:35:58.089 --> 00:36:02.059 align:start position:0%
of these sites and you know
it's not that I want there to be

00:36:02.059 --> 00:36:03.461
anything goes.

00:36:03.461 --> 00:36:06.964 align:start size:84% position:16%
I just would prefer
to put my faith

00:36:06.964 --> 00:36:09.300
in a venerable external standard

00:36:09.300 --> 00:36:12.470
like First Amendment
case law than to give

00:36:12.470 --> 00:36:15.306
all this power to
these unelected,

00:36:15.306 --> 00:36:18.042 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know oligarchs to
make these decisions about

00:36:18.042 --> 00:36:20.144
who gets to participate
in American democracy.

00:36:20.144 --> 00:36:23.347
- Yeah so let's shift
a little to lockdowns

00:36:23.347 --> 00:36:25.216 align:start size:94% position:6%
and generally what's
happened in the last year

00:36:25.216 --> 00:36:28.219
because basically from almost
the day we're posting this,

00:36:28.219 --> 00:36:30.188
it was pretty much a
year ago right now,

00:36:30.188 --> 00:36:31.889
two weeks to flatten the curve.

00:36:31.889 --> 00:36:33.591
Now it's a year
later and people,

00:36:33.591 --> 00:36:36.861 align:start position:0%
you know Biden's double-masking
and you know California,

00:36:36.861 --> 00:36:38.796
you still can't go
to indoor restaurants

00:36:38.796 --> 00:36:40.965
or go to the gym and
I guess we're gonna

00:36:40.965 --> 00:36:44.468 align:start size:94% position:6%
partially open on April
1st or something like that

00:36:44.468 --> 00:36:47.705
but so much of this does
seem attached to big tech

00:36:47.705 --> 00:36:49.507
because they're letting
you say certain things

00:36:49.507 --> 00:36:52.443 align:start position:0%
about lockdowns and not
letting you say certain things.

00:36:52.443 --> 00:36:54.712 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is your belief that
there was just no science

00:36:54.712 --> 00:36:57.381
behind the lockdowns
at all at any point?

00:36:58.549 --> 00:37:01.018 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Well I guess at the
very beginning of COVID,

00:37:01.018 --> 00:37:03.387
we didn't exactly know.

00:37:03.387 --> 00:37:06.424
So I don't fault
anyone too much for

00:37:06.424 --> 00:37:08.259
the decisions that
were made to lockdown

00:37:08.259 --> 00:37:10.995
back in March or April.

00:37:10.995 --> 00:37:14.398 align:start size:84% position:16%
We simply didn't know,
as you all recall

00:37:14.398 --> 00:37:16.334 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean we were seeing
what was happening Italy,

00:37:16.334 --> 00:37:18.469
the hospitals were
completely overrun

00:37:18.469 --> 00:37:22.707 align:start size:97% position:3%
and so this idea of you
know, of locking things down

00:37:22.707 --> 00:37:26.877 align:start position:0%
to buy time so that our medical
system didn't get overrun.

00:37:26.877 --> 00:37:30.114 align:start size:97% position:3%
I could understand the
argument but I think by this,

00:37:30.114 --> 00:37:33.618
by last summer it was
becoming pretty clear

00:37:33.618 --> 00:37:37.321
that lockdowns had an
enormous cost to them

00:37:37.321 --> 00:37:40.524
and a very unclear benefit

00:37:40.524 --> 00:37:43.194
and I think as the
year progressed,

00:37:43.194 --> 00:37:45.463 align:start size:84% position:16%
we had more and more
data around this.

00:37:45.463 --> 00:37:47.732
Certainly I think by the fall,

00:37:47.732 --> 00:37:50.935
anyone who was paying
attention to that data

00:37:50.935 --> 00:37:54.472 align:start size:97% position:3%
would've, you know, should've
been against lockdowns.

00:37:54.472 --> 00:37:58.042 align:start size:97% position:3%
I think even by May, I was
tweeting that the right policy

00:37:58.042 --> 00:38:00.845
was to go all in on masks
and to not do lockdowns.

00:38:00.845 --> 00:38:05.182 align:start size:97% position:3%
So I mean, I think the data
was clear even by then.

00:38:05.182 --> 00:38:06.150
- So what do you
think was going on?

00:38:06.150 --> 00:38:07.685
I mean in the mind,

00:38:07.685 --> 00:38:10.221
we'll get to California more
specifically but generally,

00:38:10.221 --> 00:38:11.656
in the mind of Gavin Newsom,

00:38:11.656 --> 00:38:13.724
in the mind of Gretchen Whitmer,

00:38:13.724 --> 00:38:14.959
in the mind of Andrew Cuomo.

00:38:14.959 --> 00:38:16.727 align:start size:84% position:16%
These people that
you know, in essence,

00:38:16.727 --> 00:38:19.063 align:start size:84% position:16%
destroyed their states
in a lot of ways

00:38:19.063 --> 00:38:22.566
versus Texas and scary Florida.

00:38:22.566 --> 00:38:24.669 align:start size:84% position:16%
Like what do you think
they were thinking?

00:38:24.669 --> 00:38:27.204
Because I can't find
any science anywhere.

00:38:27.204 --> 00:38:28.439
I get what you're saying, yeah.

00:38:28.439 --> 00:38:29.874
For a couple weeks
you could try it

00:38:29.874 --> 00:38:31.676 align:start size:94% position:6%
and there was this unsure
moment and all that

00:38:31.676 --> 00:38:33.444
but then after that.

00:38:33.444 --> 00:38:38.449
- Yeah I mean, I don't know
exactly why they thought

00:38:39.684 --> 00:38:42.219
the political calculus,
it would benefit them

00:38:42.219 --> 00:38:44.255 align:start size:84% position:16%
to have this extreme
lockdown policy

00:38:44.255 --> 00:38:47.191 align:start size:94% position:6%
but I think there was sort
of a collective failure

00:38:47.191 --> 00:38:52.196 align:start size:97% position:3%
of elite thinking on COVID and
really on many other issues

00:38:53.631 --> 00:38:57.101 align:start size:94% position:6%
and really, you have to
go back to the WHO getting

00:38:57.101 --> 00:38:58.436 align:start size:97% position:3%
just about everything wrong.
- Yep.

00:38:58.436 --> 00:38:59.670
- From the beginning.

00:38:59.670 --> 00:39:02.373
First you know, all the
back in January saying

00:39:02.373 --> 00:39:05.209 align:start position:0%
that it wasn't clear that there
was human to human contact

00:39:05.209 --> 00:39:08.045
and then when it was
clear that there was,

00:39:08.045 --> 00:39:12.450
they greatly exaggerated
the case fatality rate

00:39:12.450 --> 00:39:16.387
by claiming that there weren't
a lot of asymptomatic cases.

00:39:16.387 --> 00:39:19.690
You know, we would later learn
that there was a huge number

00:39:19.690 --> 00:39:22.660 align:start size:88% position:13%
of asymptomatic cases
and therefore we should,

00:39:22.660 --> 00:39:24.195 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know the infection
fatality rate

00:39:24.195 --> 00:39:27.131 line:0%
was more like one tenth
the case fatality rate

00:39:27.131 --> 00:39:29.900 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
but they got that wrong
and sort of miseducated us

00:39:29.900 --> 00:39:33.571 line:0%
and then that led to us
thinking that the virus

00:39:33.571 --> 00:39:35.873
would be even more
severe than it was.

00:39:35.873 --> 00:39:38.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, then they
were wrong about masks

00:39:38.676 --> 00:39:41.946
and so was Fauci and the CDC.

00:39:41.946 --> 00:39:44.382
I mean I wrote a blog
post in early April

00:39:44.382 --> 00:39:47.718 align:start size:84% position:16%
saying that masks
should be the policy.

00:39:47.718 --> 00:39:50.221 align:start size:88% position:13%
I thought that was, that
was the type of thing

00:39:50.221 --> 00:39:52.890
that was low cost high benefit

00:39:52.890 --> 00:39:57.461 align:start position:0%
and the type of thing we
should be doing, not lockdowns.

00:39:57.461 --> 00:40:00.564 align:start size:88% position:13%
And then you know, they
got lockdowns wrong

00:40:00.564 --> 00:40:01.999 align:start size:84% position:16%
and then they reversed
course on that

00:40:01.999 --> 00:40:03.701
And so there's just been like,

00:40:03.701 --> 00:40:07.271
systematically I think
the health establishment

00:40:07.271 --> 00:40:10.174
just seems they've gotten
everything wrong here.

00:40:10.174 --> 00:40:12.910 align:start size:84% position:16%
And it kind of, I mean
just to tie it back

00:40:12.910 --> 00:40:14.512
to the censorship thing.

00:40:14.512 --> 00:40:18.749
It shows how ridiculous it
is to maintain this position

00:40:18.749 --> 00:40:22.753
that we can censor based
on what experts tell us

